# Release Notes Dialog Compatibility Check

The initial lookup using the issue name and call name for the release notes dialog led to `src/js/background.js`. At the time, the only confirmed details were the four terms `target`, `version`, `dom`, and `returns`, along with a few direct dependencies at the top of the file:

```js
import { gsBackup } from './gsBackup.js';
import { gsChrome } from './gsChrome.js';
import { gsNewsFeed } from './gsNewsFeed.js';
import { gsSession } from './gsSession.js';
```

The first draft treated this as an entry point for switching content by version, but did not check the target node, version selection, and returned result individually. The migration sequence was initially recorded as: find the call site → check `target` and `version` → compare against the actual changes in DOM → then decide whether to replace the old approach.

On rollback, keep the old method for checking calls; do not delete the original record first. TODO: add a snippet showing the actual render call to confirm which entry point is used to maintain the release notes.

# Focus and Recovery Routing History

When RC2 was wrapped up, focus Event trailing debounce and service worker Responsiveness Recovery Routing were put in the same record. Both may appear before or after changes to the debug page, but no decision was made at the time on whether they should share a deferred action.

The initial approach was to look at them separately. For focus, first preserve the order of consecutive events, then check the final result left on the page at the trailing point; for recovery, work back from the diagnostic content and debug page. A page refresh shows only that something changed on the page side; by itself, it cannot be recorded as the service worker having resumed responding.

Earlier investigations led to `src/js/debug.js`. The file header shows:

```js
import { gsBackup } from './gsBackup.js';
import { gsChrome } from './gsChrome.js';
import { gsFavicon } from './gsFavicon.js';
import { gsIndexedDb } from './gsIndexedDb.js';
```

The objects involved here extend beyond focus alone, so this is only a place to start looking on the debugging side for now. The imports indicate that backup, browser, icon, and IndexedDB objects are all present near the debug file; that alone does not establish a direct association between any one of those objects and focus events or recovery routing. In particular, when storage differs between the before and after states, first record whether the change occurred before input or after output; don't combine the two into a single result just because the debug page also changed.

The next check needs to capture page results separately for successful and failed calls after consecutive focus events have been merged. For failures, don't just record "not recovered" or "no change"; the record should also show whether there were any other adjacent focus events beforehand. For recovery, we still need a set of materials to check whether the diagnostic content, debug page, and before/after storage states line up. At this stage, these locations are only starting points for checking what happened; don't assign them a confirmed execution order.
